/* The comparison "table". Built as a real <table> so it lays out as a grid,
     but every cell is a plain <td>. The first ROW (plan names) and the first
     COLUMN (feature names) are styled to read unmistakably as headers. */

/* ---- markup pasted straight from a spreadsheet export ----
     The export wrapped the range in <table> and emitted class-laden <td>s.
     The header row got its OWN style classes (bold, fill) but is still <td>. */

/* A React-style data grid built from divs with explicit ARIA table roles.
     role=table / role=row / role=cell are all present and valid — BUT the first
     row and first column are role="cell", never role="columnheader"/"rowheader". */

/* The article body and the callout are flex siblings. CSS `order` floats the
     callout to the right margin VISUALLY, but in the DOM it is wedged between the
     two halves of one sentence (p#part-a and p#part-b). */

/* The museum's web officer rebuilt the page in a flex layout and used `order` to pull the
     practical "Visit" box to the left rail on wide screens, leaving the HTML in
     content-first order (essay, then the box) for the mobile stack. */

/* The license body is dumped as a single monospaced, white-space-preserving
       block. Two "columns" are created purely by padding the left clause with
       spaces so the right clause starts at a fixed character column. */

/* The whole specials grid is one preformatted, monospaced blob. The column
       alignment (Breakfast / Lunch / Dinner) and the row labels (the weekdays)
       are produced ONLY by space characters. No <table>, <th>, scope, or role. */

/* The "ready to paste" signature is a read-only textarea. The author aligned
       a 3-column contact card (Name | Title | Phone) with literal TAB characters
       plus tab-size so it looks like a tidy directory grid in a monospace font. */

/* The itinerary is a read-only textarea. The author space-aligned six columns
       (Flight / Date / From->To / Depart / Arrive / Gate) so it looks like the
       confirmation grids airlines email. No <table>, no role — spaces only. */

/* "Put the most-read clause on top and tighten spacing on phones."
         column-reverse REVERSES the visual order of the legally-ordered sub-clauses
         to 7.3, 7.2, 7.1 while source order stays 7.1, 7.2, 7.3. */

/* "Reverse the transport so the thumb reaches the buttons better on phones."
         This flips visual order to Next | Play | Previous, contradicting the caption's
         stated left-to-right: previous, play, next — and inverting back/forward. */

/* Push the decorative banner to the bottom on phones so the numbers come first.
         The three KPI cards are independent statistics; their relative order does not
         affect meaning. No order-dependent sequence exists here. */

/* === The defect ===
     valid field: 2px gray border.  invalid field: 2px red border.
     The two hues are tuned to nearly the SAME lightness so grayscale can't tell them apart. */

/* === the defect ===
     When confirm != new, JS adds .mismatch which paints a red outline ONLY.
     The hint below is unconditional instruction text — it is not an error message. */

/* === the defect ===
     invalid field gets a red border. The aria-invalid="true" attribute is ALSO set,
     but that is a programmatic-only channel; there is NO visible non-color cue. */

/* NOTE: the card is NOT a scroll/clip container (no overflow set), so the over-wide
     stats bar actually widens the card itself — it is not hidden or given its own
     scrollbar. The card becomes too wide for the 320px viewport. */

/* RESPONSIVE: stack to a single column on small viewports.
     The article reflows perfectly. But the related-investigations rail is
     removed entirely — and its links live nowhere else on the page. */

/* This is the PASS control. Same kind of long opaque tracking URL as case-01, shown as the
     full link text — but here the author applied overflow-wrap:break-word (plus the modern
     anywhere fallback) on the link. At 320px the URL wraps cleanly inside the fluid column;
     no horizontal scrollbar appears. Nothing is truncated and the full value remains the
     accessible name of the link. */

/* This is the renderer's stock prose/code styling. It is what every Markdown-to-HTML
       converter ships: code blocks do not wrap, they scroll. Perfectly fine for code; a
       barrier for prose that got mis-detected as code. */

/* Author reduced the per-level indent at the narrow breakpoint (the G224 indentation advice) —
       but left white-space:nowrap intact, so the item TEXT still cannot reflow. The min-width comment
       is a red herring: it is never paired with wrapping. */

/* The masthead is a position:sticky header roughly 130px tall. At a desktop
       height (900px+) this is a reasonable 14% of the screen. At the Reflow viewport
       height of 256px it consumes ~51% of the screen, leaving a sliver to read in. */

/* FIXED top nav ~92px. FIXED bottom consent bar ~96px. Together ~188px of fixed
       chrome. At a 256px-tall reflow viewport that leaves ~68px of scrollable content
       window — a thin strip. At desktop height (900px) the same two bars are unremarkable. */

/* The consent bar is fixed to the bottom and — critically — is NOT dismissible.
       It has an "Accept" button that sets a flag, but the markup gives no "close"/"reject"
       affordance that REMOVES the bar, so it permanently consumes the bottom of the viewport. */

/* The close control is a tiny X in the corner, and — because it is the LAST focusable
       element in the DOM — a keyboard user reaches it only after tabbing through the whole
       article and the ad's CTA. It also relies on a 16px hit area. */

/* FAIL control: borderless, distinguished ONLY by a very pale blue fill (#EAF2FB)
     against the white page = 1.13:1. The fill is the only cue that an input exists,
     and it is far below 3:1. */

/* FAIL pill: outline #8E9BA6 vs page = 2.62:1 (< 3:1).
     Visually almost identical to the .ok pill, but below threshold — and since the pill's
     interior matches the page, this outline is the ONLY thing that says "control here." */

/* ICON-ONLY buttons. The glyphs are #788294 on the #5b6675 bar == 1.50:1.
     There is no text, no visible boundary; the faint icon is the ONLY thing that
     signals a control is there. Below 3:1 -> FAIL. */

/* TEXT-LABELLED button. It carries the SAME faint #788294 icon at the SAME
     1.50:1, but the white (5.83:1) text label "Comment" identifies the control,
     so the low-contrast icon is not required to identify presence -> PASS. */

/* Both money inputs share the SAME faint outline.
     #A8A8A8 on #FFFFFF == 2.38:1 (below 3:1). In RTL the currency affordance
     and label mirror to the right, but the contrast number is layout-agnostic. */

/* PASS field: a visible right-aligned "ر.س" currency adornment (high contrast,
     #14532d == 9.11:1) plus its label clearly identify the amount control. The
     faint border is therefore not the only cue. */

/* FAIL field: the "other amount" box is a bare white box with no adornment,
     no placeholder, no visible label text (label is sr-only). Its only cue is
     the same 2.38:1 border. */

/* the right-edge "close editor" SYMBOLIC glyph button (✕) — symbolic char,
     low contrast => non-text content failing 3:1. NOTE: in RTL the close sits
     on the LEFT visually but is the logical end. */

/* The segmented radio "buttons". The selected state and the focus state both
     use a 3px solid teal border — so a focused-but-unselected segment looks the
     same as the selected one, and focus is not distinguishable from selection. */

/* The "Confirm" button is a SOLID blue primary button. Its interior fill IS blue
     #4189B9, and the 2px blue border traces its visible edge (border same hue as fill,
     so the resting boundary reads as one solid blue shape on the white card). */

/* THE DEFECT: focus recolours the SAME border in place (within the visible boundary)
     from blue to green. Green #4B933A vs the blue interior #4189B9 = 1.005:1 -> fails.
     No outer outline is added. */

/* The thumb. On a switch the THUMB'S POSITION is the state indicator: left = OFF,
     right = ON. So the thumb is the "visual information necessary to indicate state",
     and its adjacent color is the track it sits on (another part of the component). */

/* The chevron is the ONLY graphical cue of expanded vs collapsed: it points
     right when closed and rotates down when open. The chevron color #8A96A6 was
     chosen as a "soft" slate; against the #5C6B7E header band it is 1.81:1. */

/* The selection indicator: a 4px left accent bar. Unselected rows show the
       row background through it; selected rows get the accent bar painted.
       Row background is #EEF2F7 (zebra) / #FFFFFF. */

/* SELECTED state = the left accent bar is painted #9FB4CF.
     On the #EEF2F7 zebra row that bar is 1.89:1; on the #FFFFFF row it's even
     fainter. The accent bar is the ONLY signal that a row is shortlisted. */

/* Selected rows are pinned to the #EEF2F7 surface so the ONLY thing that
     differs from an unselected row is the painted accent bar (no background
     change carries the state). #9FB4CF bar on #EEF2F7 = 1.89:1. */

/* The status pill carries an info icon. On hover it shows a hint that LOOKS like
     the browser's native title tooltip: same dark gray, same small rounded corners,
     same font, same ~12px size, positioned just below. But it is an author DIV. */

/* Accessibility/feature icon. The author explicitly sets title="" to KILL the
     browser's native tooltip, then paints an identical-looking gray bubble using a
     CSS ::after that pulls its text from a data-* attribute via attr(). No JS. */

/* The bubble appears ONLY while the 22px icon itself is hovered. A ::after pseudo-
     element is not in the accessibility tree, cannot receive the pointer, and there
     is no Esc affordance — so it is neither hoverable nor dismissible. */

/* AUTHOR overlay tooltip. Appears on hover of a bar, positioned floating near the
     cursor anchor. It shows a RICHER, DIFFERENT string than the SVG <title>. It is
     pointer-events:none and has no Esc handler. */

/* Modal dialog backdrop + box. This dialog is opened when the high-risk amount
     field RECEIVES FOCUS (a security confirmation). It is modal: it traps focus,
     dims the page, and must be acknowledged. */

/* Tooltip opens DOWNWARD from the conditions line and overlaps the symbol-key row below.
     It is wired to be hoverable + persistent (see script): it overlaps its trigger row and
     the pointer can travel onto it. */

/* The popover is pushed AWAY from the large trigger (to its right, into the next column),
     so it does not overlap the trigger — but it now lands squarely on the "Monthly cost
     estimate" breakdown and appears far from where the user is looking/pointing. */

/* Reveal ONLY while the category itself is hovered/focused. Nothing bridges the
     20px corridor, and the flyout's taller height means lower items require a
     diagonal path that leaves both boxes. */

/* Glossary abbreviation styled with a hover-only CSS tooltip.
     This is PURE CSS — there is no JS at all, and CSS :hover cannot
     respond to keyboard focus on a non-focusable <abbr>. */

/* Color swatches. Each swatch shows the color name on hover. The name is ALSO
     printed as a visible <span> right next to the swatch and announced via the
     radio's <label>, so the hover bubble is purely redundant. */

/* Glossary term: a focusable <button> styled inline, with a tooltip bound to BOTH
     hover AND focus, dismissible via Esc, and hoverable (the popup itself can be
     entered without it closing). This is a complete, conforming implementation. */

/* The giant pale word floats across the UPPER scene as a mood flourish.
     It is positioned AWAY from the solid copy panel so it composites only
     against the light/mid scene (≈1.4–2:1) — unambiguously low-contrast. */

/* All INFORMATIVE text lives on a SOLID opaque dark panel, so every
     in-scope string is white-on-#14201c and contrast is CSSOM-computable
     (no photo sampling, no text-shadow guesswork). */

/* The price row. The currency unit and "/mo" are dark and pass.
     The NUMBER itself is set to a pale gray "accent" — the only place the
     actual price magnitude is shown. #a3a3a3 on #fff = 2.52:1. */

/* Ornamental rule between sections: a faint repeated word used purely as a
     decorative divider line. The repeated token "menu" carries no information —
     the real section name is the <h2> right below it. ~1.51:1 by design. */

/* A dark "navigation rail" the marketing dev added down the left side. It is the
     card's POSITIONED ANCESTOR, so a CSSOM background-walker that climbs the box tree
     from the card stops HERE and sees #1b1b1b (near-black). */

/* The failing text. Its DECLARED color is near-black (#262626); against the light
     #c0c0c0 panel that computes to ~8.3:1 -> a confident CSS pass. The blend mode is
     what changes the RENDERED color. */

/* The metadata strip below the art is normal, legible, and NOT part of the defect:
     opaque dark bar, light text, no blend mode. It shows the same card can be fine
     when nothing is composited. */

/* The wordmark renders via SVG <text> with a rose-gold linearGradient fill on
       white. Least-contrast pixel (the lighter stop #e0b483 on #fff) ≈ 1.91:1;
       the darker stop #d9a86b ≈ 2.15:1. Both are below 4.5:1 AND below the 3:1
       large-text threshold. The mark is ~34px ≈ 25.5pt, i.e. large-scale text. */

/* The brand TAGLINE / slogan, treated by the agency as "part of our brand identity"
       and therefore (wrongly) given the same exemption as the logo. Muted gold
       #bfa86f on the cream footer #f6f3ee ≈ 2.10:1. This is a slogan, not the logotype. */

/* The "BETA" pill sits inside the lockup, so it LOOKS like part of the logo. But it is
       a product STATUS label (this portal is in beta), not the brand mark. Muted gold
       #b6a45f on white ≈ 2.48:1 — below 4.5:1. */

/* === IN-SCOPE FAILURE: postcode field has a GRADIENT fill (no single bg color). ===
     Placeholder grey #9aa0a6 over this gradient = ~2.48:1 (light end) .. ~1.53:1 (deep end).
     The placeholder states the REQUIRED format, so it is meaningful, in-scope text. */

/* === DECORATIVE (EXCLUDED): newsletter "vibe" filler, faint grey on white (~1.66:1).
     Conveys no information; could be removed/rearranged without changing meaning. ===
     A color-only checker would mis-flag this; the human carve-out excludes it. */

/* The pull-quote is a "dynamically generated image" served by the CMS quote engine.
     A size control re-requests the image at a larger size — but the engine returns the
     SAME small raster and the larger sizes are just CSS-upscaled (pixelated). */

/* The site name is an IMAGE OF TEXT: an SVG wordmark delivered via background-image.
     The <h1>'s real text content is the sr-only decoy below, so checkers see a named
     heading; the displayed glyphs are background pixels, not live text. */

/* Custom "Review transfer" control: a span styled as the primary button. It is
     focusable and activates with Enter. It does NOT respond to Space — which, per the
     WCAG Understanding note, still SATISFIES 2.1.1 for a custom control. */

/* The submenu panel. It is in the DOM, but only DISPLAYED when the parent
     <li> is HOVERED. There is no :focus-within rule, so it never opens for a
     keyboard user tabbing through the top-level links. */

/* Row action buttons are HIDDEN until the row is hovered. They are real <button>
     elements (in the DOM, focusable in principle), but visibility:hidden + the
     hover reveal is the only way they ever surface to the user. */

/* Ingredient amounts carry a JS mouseover tooltip showing the METRIC conversion.
     This is a convenience reveal; the same conversions are ALSO printed, as visible
     text, in the "Metric conversions" table further down the page. */

/* F10: swallow Tab / Shift+Tab inside this embedded document and re-grab focus to
       the only control, so focus can never carry out of the iframe back to the parent
       shell. No Esc handler, no exit advice anywhere in this document or the host. */

/* Sibling headings keep the bar-chart icon as an ACCENT beside real words (and aria-hidden,
     so it adds nothing to their name), keeping the iconic look consistent so the wordless
     nutrition heading blends visually in with the rest. */

/* The donation aside is FIRST in the DOM but painted on the RIGHT via order:2;
     the main course content is SECOND in the DOM but painted LEFT via order:1.
     No tabindex, no JS. The two regions are unrelated. */

/* All three step panels live in one grid. grid-row controls the visual top-to-bottom
     position: Step 1 -> row 1, Step 2 -> row 2, Step 3 -> row 3. But the DOM order of the
     panels is 3, 1, 2. No tabindex, no JS, so Tab follows DOM: Step 3 first. */

/* The reply form is appended at the END of the whole thread, then floated visually
     under the comment via absolute positioning is NOT used here — it simply renders at
     the bottom but is shown inline-styled to look like it belongs to the first comment. */

/* The whole form is ONE CSS grid. Two columns (billing | shipping); rows for the
     section heading + the five address lines. Each field is pinned to a named cell, so
     visual placement is independent of DOM source order. */

/* Two real fieldsets, side by side. Because each fieldset contains its OWN four fields
     in order, the natural DOM source order is applicant-group-complete then
     co-applicant-group-complete — the passing G59 ordering. No grid re-routing needed. */

/* One CSS grid for both attendee cards. In RTL the FIRST grid column is on the RIGHT, so
     "att1" (right) before "att2" (left) matches the reading direction. Each field is
     pinned by named area; DOM order is interleaved between the two attendees. */

/* Single-column form, two stacked sections. One CSS grid with named rows; each field is
     pinned to its section's row so the DOM (interleaved) order is decoupled from the
     visual (section-blocked) order. */

/* A "design accent" the marketing team wanted between fields. The author made it
       tabindex="0" so they could attach a scroll-reveal animation that triggers on focus.
       It is purely decorative text but now sits IN the tab sequence, mid-form. */

/* PASS element #1: a status region placed at a LOGICAL point — right after the submit
       button, where any save result belongs. It is focusable so we can move focus to it on
       save (so the user lands on the confirmation). It never interrupts a data-entry chain. */

/* PASS element #2: a read-only summary placed at the very END of the form, AFTER all
       inputs and the submit button. It is focusable (tabindex="0") so users can review it,
       but it adds no mid-sequence stop. */

/* A SECOND header-LOOKING row built from <td> styled to mimic <th>. It visually labels the
       download columns ("Specifications", "Bid Form") but is NOT a th and is NOT in any header
       scope, so it is not an associated header cell for the links below it. */

/* The product NAME/DESC and the action LINK are two SEPARATE sibling <div>s inside the card.
       They are visually stacked so they read as one unit, but the <a> ("View") shares no
       sentence/paragraph/list-item/cell with the description, and there is no aria association. */

/* These look exactly like section headings — big, bold, dark — but they are
       <p class="invoice-title">, NOT <h2>/<h3>. They are not in the heading tree
       and (critically) are in a different element from the action links below. */

/* The product detail sits in a two-column grid. The author named the areas
     and then, for visual balance, swapped which named area each panel renders
     into — so the SHIPPING panel is laid out in the area visually above REVIEWS. */

/* DOM order: feesHead(1), feesBody(2), contactHead(3), contactBody(4).
     order values rearrange the VISUAL column to:
       feesHead → contactBody → contactHead → feesBody  */

/* Each seat is a CUSTOM widget: div[tabindex][role=button], NOT a native control.
     The sole focus cue is a background-COLOR swap. Native outline removed (divs have
     none by default anyway). No border-color change, no box-shadow, no outline. */

/* ---- defect part (1)+(2): the ONLY "current option" cue is this pale bg tint,
     applied by JS to a sibling div. No focus moves, no aria-activedescendant. The pale
     tint also flattens to system Canvas in forced-colors mode. ---- */

/* box-shadow for the soft look + a transparent outline that forced-colors turns into a
     real system-colored ring. The box-shadow may be dropped in forced colors; the outline
     survives. */

/* The article's inline links AND the kicker nav links. The author wanted a
     "subtle, editorial" focus treatment and chose a 1-shade-off ink color.
     No underline change, no outline, no weight change — pure near-black shift. */

/* Author-supplied focus ring — BLACK, same hue as the 4px black border and
       thinner than it, so it is drawn against / absorbed by the resting frame.
       (Not outline:none; a real :focus rule exists, so scanners see a style.) */

/* Author-supplied, clearly distinguishing focus indicator: a thick solid
       amber ring with offset + colour shift, unlike any tab's resting dotted
       box. Removes the dotted style on focus and replaces it. */

/* Full-bleed footer: negative margins cancel the body's 22px side padding so
     the buttons reach the surface's left/right edges and bottom, sitting in the
     18px-rounded bottom corners. */

/* Author focus ring — present and high contrast. It IS drawn on each drawer
     link; it is simply painted off-screen when the drawer is in its closed
     (translated-off) state. */

/* MITIGATION 1: the scroll container reserves space at the bottom equal to
     the fixed footer, so any scroll-into-view for a focused field stops above
     the footer rather than under it. */

/* MITIGATION 2: each focusable field carries scroll-margin so when it is
     scrolled into view there is a clear gap above the footer where its ring
     can be seen. */

/* The real focusable element is THIS link. Its UA outline was stripped so the
       "tile lift" reads as the only feedback. The lift renders on the WRAPPER,
       far from where the focus actually is, and is visually identical to hover. */

/* The filter chips are real <button>s. The original CSS reset killed the
       focus outline for buttons globally; the author later "re-added" feedback
       in BOTH :hover and :focus so it would look styled in either state. */

/* The CTA buttons. Utility-class mindset: kill the default ring
       (`focus:outline-none`) and add only a press-feedback transform
       (`active:scale-95`). No focus/focus-visible utility was ever added. */

/* The date-format hint, built as a "floating help bar" pinned to the bottom of the viewport.
     display:none by default; becomes display:block (and visible) when the input is focused (the
     focus reveal genuinely fires). Its z-index is LOWER than the summary bar's. */

/* The fixed booking-summary footer. position:fixed, pinned to bottom:0, OPAQUE, z-index HIGHER
     than the help bar. Both bars occupy the bottom of the viewport, so the revealed help bar always
     paints directly behind this footer — in every viewport size — and the footer fully occludes it. */

/* Focus-revealed format instruction. Hidden by default; revealed correctly on keyboard focus
     (focus-within / :focus), on hover, AND whenever the field holds a value. In normal flow, fully
     visible, no overlay, no timer. */

/* Visually-hidden helper. The element stays in the DOM and in the accessibility tree
     (it is NOT display:none and NOT aria-hidden), so a screen reader reading the input's
     description will speak it — but it occupies a 1px clipped box and is invisible on screen. */

/* The strength meter. Four segments; weak passwords light only the first, in red.
     This colour-and-fill is the ENTIRE guidance about how to make the password
     acceptable — there is no text listing the rules. */

/* The error icon. It is swapped in next to the field label on validation failure.
     Its alt/aria are intentionally empty so it is purely decorative to AT — the visual
     triangle is the entire error+correction cue, and it carries no format guidance. */

/* The required marker: a red asterisk, placed by CSS, with no text legend explaining
     it. On the errored field it INTENSIFIES (darker, bolder) — colour/weight is the only
     change that signals "this is the problem field." */

/* The error message: same red as the icon, programmatically tied to the input via
     aria-describedby, and it STATES THE CORRECTION (the required format + an example).
     This is the only structural difference from the failing icon-only twins. */
